# docs/KEYMAP.md §Native agent thread — an agent tab keeps its own controls and repeats the whole
#   Workspace session table: selection (`^s 1`–`9`), MRU (`^s Tab`, `^s w`) and `^s s` to the Hub.
# docs/KEYMAP.md §Native agent thread — an unbound `^s <key>` is swallowed with a toast and never
#   reaches the composer.
# docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md §4 — a picker with nothing to offer draws nothing
fixture: agents
await idle
key o
await screen == Workspace 20000
key ctrl-s a
await agents.threads[0].provider == claude 20000
assert focused == agents.composer
await idle 30000

# An unbound `^s` second key runs nothing and — the regression this file exists for — types
# nothing into the draft: gpui replays an unmatched sequence as input, so leaving the chord to it
# put a stray character in the composer. The oracle is the tab label below, which is this thread's
# first message and would carry the stray `q` with it.
key ctrl-s q
await toasts[0] exists 5000
assert toasts[0].text ~= "not bound here"
assert screen == Workspace && mode == Agent && key_contexts[1] == AgentIdle

type what does this crate do
key enter
await agents.threads[0].state == working 20000
await agents.threads[0].state == idle 40000
assert lists.tabs.selected.label == "what does this crate do"

# `^s [` freezes the tail and enters scroll mode; `q` leaves it and re-arms the follow.
key ctrl-s [
await mode == Scroll 10000
assert key_contexts[0] == Agent && key_contexts[1] == AgentNativeScroll
shot scroll-mode
key q
await mode == Agent 10000
assert key_contexts[1] == AgentIdle

# `^s m` opens the model picker. It paints no target and no snapshot field, so the oracle is
# the one a user feels: an open picker takes `Enter`, so the composer's draft is *not* sent.
type a draft that must survive
key ctrl-s m
key enter
assert agents.threads[0].state == idle && lists.tabs.selected.label == "what does this crate do"

# Numeric selection leaves the agent tab, and selecting its fourth Workspace slot returns with
# composer focus. Selecting two terminals first gives the terminal MRU a real alternate;
# `^s Tab` from the agent must then return to that alternate terminal.
key ctrl-s 2
await mode == Terminal 10000
await lists.tabs.selected.id == 2 10000
key ctrl-s 1
await lists.tabs.selected.id == 1 10000
key ctrl-s 4
await mode == Agent 10000
assert focused == agents.composer
key ctrl-s tab
await mode == Terminal 10000
key ctrl-s 4
await mode == Agent 10000
assert focused == agents.composer

# The session-MRU route is also available from the agent context. The fixture may have a hidden
# process session in its MRU, so the stable contract here is that the native view stays usable.
key ctrl-s w
wait 200
assert screen == Workspace && mode == Agent && focused == agents.composer

# `^s s` is a Workspace session row, and an agent tab repeats the session table: it leaves for the
# Hub with the thread still running, exactly as it does over a terminal. `o` comes straight back
# to the same worktree, and its agent tab is still the selected one.
key ctrl-s s
await screen == Hub 10000
assert key_contexts[0] == Hub
key o
await screen == Workspace 20000
await mode == Agent 10000
assert key_contexts[1] == AgentIdle

# `^s F` is the terminal fallback: the floating PTY popup over the same worktree.
key ctrl-s F
await agents.popup == Terminal 15000
assert key_contexts[0] == Agent && key_contexts[1] == Terminal
key ctrl-s q
await agents.popup absent 10000
assert mode == Agent && key_contexts[1] == AgentIdle

# `^s x` closes the tab and hands the Workspace back to the process tab beside it.
key ctrl-s x
await mode == Terminal 15000
assert lists.tabs.rows[3] absent && screen == Workspace
dump closed
quit
